---
// Written for a sceptical psychology lecturer. The tables come from the
// instrument and norms files, so they cannot drift from what the tests use.
import LongForm from '../layouts/LongForm.astro';
import { INSTRUMENTS, TEST_PATHS } from '../lib/instruments';

const withNorms = INSTRUMENTS.filter((i) => i.norms);
const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-GB');
const sections = [
	{ id: 'instruments', title: 'Which instruments, and why these' },
	{ id: 'scoring', title: 'How each instrument is scored' },
	{ id: 'norms', title: 'Where the norms come from' },
	{ id: 'percentiles', title: 'How percentiles are calculated' },
	{ id: 'reliability', title: 'Reliability per scale' },
	{ id: 'limits', title: 'The known limits of self-report' },
	{ id: 'storage', title: 'What is stored where' },
];
---

<LongForm title="Methodology: how the tests are scored and compared" description="How every instrument on the site is scored, where each norm table comes from with sample sizes, the reliability per scale, how percentiles are calculated, the limits of self-report, and what is stored where." heading="Methodology" subtitle="How the tests are scored, compared and stored, with the figures" sections={sections} updatedLabel="Last reviewed">
	<p slot="lead">Everything here is checked by the build: the item counts and keys are asserted against the published keys, the norm tables are generated by committed scripts from named public datasets with the input file's hash recorded, and the figures below are read from the same files the tests use.</p>

	<section id="instruments">
		<h2>Which instruments, and why these</h2>
		<p>Every instrument is a published measure of a trait or an interest whose licence allows any use: the International Personality Item Pool (public domain), the Ten-Item Personality Inventory (free for any use by its author), the Rosenberg Self-Esteem Scale (public domain) and the O*NET Interest Profiler Short Form (Creative Commons Attribution-NoDerivatives, reproduced without change). No instrument with a research-only, non-commercial or unclear licence is used, and nothing that screens for a condition. Items are reproduced character for character and the instrument is named as its authors name it.</p>
		<div class="table-scroll"><table>
			<thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th class="n">Items</th><th class="n">Scales</th><th>Licence</th><th>Used on</th></tr></thead>
			<tbody>
				{INSTRUMENTS.map(({ instrument }) => (
					<tr><td>{instrument.name}</td><td class="n">{instrument.items.length}</td><td class="n">{instrument.scales.filter((s) => !s.parent).length}</td><td>{instrument.licence.text.split('.')[0]}</td><td>{TEST_PATHS[instrument.id] ? <a href={TEST_PATHS[instrument.id]}>{TEST_PATHS[instrument.id]}</a> : <span class="quiet">not yet</span>}</td></tr>
				))}
			</tbody>
		</table></div>
	</section>

	<section id="scoring">
		<h2>How each instrument is scored</h2>
		<p>Scoring is a pure function in the open engine: the instrument file lists each item with its scale and its key (+1 or -1), and the response scale. A positively keyed item scores the option number chosen, counting from the lowest-scoring option; a negatively keyed item scores points plus one minus that number. A scale score is the sum (or for the TIPI the mean) of its items. A domain made of facets, as in the IPIP-NEO-120, sums all its facets' items. The O*NET profiler is a tick list: each tick counts one, exactly as the paper form is self-scored. The Rosenberg scale is published with Strongly agree first, so the option order is flipped before keying, and the site scores it from 10 to 40, one of the two conventions its owner describes. Nothing is weighted, transformed or adjusted.</p>
		<p>A test in the repository asserts every file against its published key: the item count, each item's scale, and the set of reverse-keyed items. Worked examples (all highest, all lowest, all neutral, the pattern that reaches the maximum on every scale) are checked for each instrument.</p>
	</section>

	<section id="norms">
		<h2>Where the norms come from</h2>
		<p>No norm is estimated or invented. A norm table is a count of people at each possible raw score, per scale and per group, generated once by a committed script from a named public dataset. The script records the SHA-256 of the input file, so anyone can download the same file and reproduce the table. Instruments without a published dataset show raw scores against the possible range and say so on the page.</p>
		{withNorms.map(({ instrument, norms }) => (
			<Fragment>
				<h3>{instrument.name}</h3>
				<p>{norms!.source.name}. <a href={norms!.source.url} rel="noopener">{norms!.source.url}</a>. Generated {norms!.source.generated} by {norms!.source.script}; input SHA-256 {norms!.source.inputSha256}.</p>
				{norms!.source.notes && <p>{norms!.source.notes}</p>}
				<div class="table-scroll"><table>
					<thead><tr><th>Group</th><th class="n">People</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>{Object.values(norms!.groups).map((g) => <tr><td>{g.label}</td><td class="n">{fmt(g.n)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
				</table></div>
				{Object.keys(norms!.groups).some((k) => k.startsWith('age-')) && <p class="small quiet">The age bands start at 18. Every person in the dataset gave an age, and the {fmt(norms!.groups.all.n - Object.entries(norms!.groups).filter(([k]) => k.startsWith('age-')).reduce((sum, [, g]) => sum + g.n, 0))} who were under 18 are counted in the everyone group only, which is why the bands sum to fewer than the whole.</p>}
			</Fragment>
		))}
	</section>

	<section id="percentiles">
		<h2>How percentiles are calculated</h2>
		<p>A percentile rank is the share of the norm group scoring below the person plus half the share scoring the same, times 100: the mid-rank definition, so a score in the middle of a symmetric sample is the 50th. The strip draws the share of the group at each raw score as a curve, with the middle 50% (the 25th to the 75th percentile scores) darker. Curve height is relative to a flat distribution over the same scores, so spread can be compared between strips of the same instrument. By default a result is compared with everyone in the sample; the tables also hold age bands and country groups where the dataset records them.</p>
	</section>

	<section id="reliability">
		<h2>Reliability per scale</h2>
		<p>Published figures come from the paper or key page the instrument file names, and each file records where. Where the generating script could compute coefficient alpha from the norm dataset itself, that figure is shown separately and labelled as computed, never in place of a published one.</p>
		{INSTRUMENTS.map(({ instrument, norms }) => (
			<Fragment>
				<h3>{instrument.name}</h3>
				{instrument.reliability ? (
					<Fragment>
						<div class="table-scroll"><table>
							<thead><tr><th>Scale</th><th class="n">Published alpha</th>{norms?.computed && <th class="n">Computed alpha</th>}</tr></thead>
							<tbody>
								{instrument.scales.map((s) => (
									<tr><td>{s.parent ? `${s.name} (facet)` : s.name}</td><td class="n">{instrument.reliability?.[s.id] !== undefined ? instrument.reliability[s.id].toFixed(2) : 'not published'}</td>{norms?.computed && <td class="n">{norms.computed.alpha[s.id] !== undefined ? norms.computed.alpha[s.id].toFixed(2) : ''}</td>}</tr>
								))}
							</tbody>
						</table></div>
						<p class="small quiet">{instrument.reliabilitySource}{norms?.computed && ` ${norms.computed.note}`}</p>
					</Fragment>
				) : (
					<p>No single reliability figure is published for this instrument in a form the file can carry; the test page quotes what its source says.</p>
				)}
			</Fragment>
		))}
	</section>

	<section id="limits">
		<h2>The known limits of self-report</h2>
		<p>Every instrument here asks people to describe themselves. That measures self-image as much as behaviour, is shaped by mood on the day and by how the person wants to be seen, and can be faked in either direction by anyone who wants to. The norm samples are people who chose to take a test online and are younger, more female and more English-speaking than any national population; they are not representative samples, and the site says so on every page that uses them. Short scales are coarse: a two-item TIPI scale or a four-item facet moves more between attempts than a 24-item domain. Interests are not abilities, traits are not destinies, and a percentile is a comparison with a sample, not a judgement.</p>
	</section>

	<section id="storage">
		<h2>What is stored where</h2>
		<p>Responses and results are stored only in the browser's local storage on the device used, under versioned keys, so a changed instrument file never mis-scores an old attempt. Nothing about answers or results is sent to this site: there is no server-side scoring, no account and no cookies. Page views are counted with Google Analytics in its consent mode with storage denied, as the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> says. A share link encodes the scores in the link itself, after the hash, which browsers never send to a server; whoever receives the link sees the scores. Deleting the browser's site data deletes everything.</p>
		<p>Teachers and researchers: the <a href="/for-teachers-and-researchers/">page for teachers and researchers</a> says how to use the instruments in teaching or research, with the licence for each, and how to cite the site and the instruments.</p>
	</section>
</LongForm>
